# Add calibrated page marks and bilingual text layout diagnostics

## Scope and existing evidence

Implement seeded measured ruler marks, English/Spanish line breaking and
explicit missing-glyph warnings above the minimal bilingual inventory.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P2-002 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-008 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-009 — archived semantic design

## Unicode whitespace in the native glyph coverage audit (partial)

The read-only `atrament-profiles glyphs --audit-text` previously counted
non-ASCII whitespace (including U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F) as invisible,
reporting zero missing glyphs for samples that `notebook --place-text`
could not render. The native audit now skips only the compositor's implicit
ASCII space and structural control-character line breaks. Other Unicode
spaces retain their exact grapheme identities and report missing authored
strokes; there is no automatic ink, fallback or normalization.

Two native CLI regressions audit all three distinct spaces, verify that
ASCII spaces and source tabs/newlines do not inflate missing counts, and
confirm selected PDF and PNG outputs stay byte-identical when the native
compositor refuses unauthored Unicode ink. Explicitly authored versions
also produce real vector PDF strokes. This tightens diagnostics but does
not complete seeded ruler marks or bilingual line breaking in P2-013.

## Exact Unicode whitespace in statistics inventory (partial)

The separate `handwriting --inventory-text` diagnostic now uses the same
exact-ink whitespace distinction as the `glyphs --audit-text` command and
native flow compositor. Only implicit ASCII space and structural control
breaks are skipped; U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F each appear as distinct
outside-baseline graphemes, retaining explicit per-glyph statistical
mutation labels. Unicode boundary traversal proceeds from remaining text
suffixes, avoiding repeated prefix scans on a bounded 4,095-byte example.

The Rust CLI regression exercises an override, repeated graphemes,
source tabs/newlines, a single Unicode-space input, and a long bounded
sequence. A further refusal case rejects text containing only implicit
ASCII spaces or source control characters, which previously produced a
misleading zero-grapheme report; one exact authored Unicode space remains
valid. This remains a read-only diagnostics report, not authored ink,
font generation, language-specific line breaking or a render guarantee.

## Single Unicode-space glyph audit admission (partial)

The reusable `glyphs --audit-text` diagnostic also admits input consisting
solely of an exact non-ASCII space glyph. Previously its all-whitespace
precheck refused such text even though a longer string including the same
glyph was auditable and native export accepted its authored strokes.
U+00A0, U+2009 and U+202F now each report their distinct missing or
explicitly authored pen shapes on their own. ASCII-space-only and
control-break-only sources still refuse; the audit remains read-only and
does not invent a font or replace a saved glyph library.

## Explicit structural breaks in coverage audits (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native `glyphs --audit-text` and `handwriting --inventory-text`
commands now reject vertical tab, form feed and U+0085 NEL even within
otherwise visible text. Those control breaks were previously admitted
as whitespace, then silently omitted from exact coverage reports, while
`notebook --place-text` refuses them outright. Both read-only diagnostics
continue accepting ASCII space, tabs, CRLF and LF as source structure and
report exact non-ASCII space glyphs separately. A focused regression
checks both refusal and the accepted CRLF/LF/tab control case.

A follow-up also rejects unpaired carriage returns at the start, middle
or end of otherwise visible audit text. The native Markdown/plain-text
flow importer already refuses bare CR, so both diagnostics now use the
same line-ending boundary. Paired CRLF and plain LF remain valid.

This is input-parity hardening, not general bilingual layout or printed marks.

## Unicode separators in glyph coverage (partial) — 2026-11-04

The read-only native `glyphs --audit-text` and
`handwriting --inventory-text` reports now recognize U+2028 line separators
and U+2029 paragraph separators as *structural*, matching the native
Markdown/plain-text flow importer. Previously both reports listed those
source row boundaries as outside-baseline or missing authored glyphs,
misleading users about a bilingual source's actual ink coverage.

One red-before/green-after Rust regression proves both reports exclude
those separators while keeping composed `ñ`, decomposed `n` plus tilde,
U+00A0 nonbreaking spaces and U+2009 thin spaces distinct. Purely
structural-only sources still refuse rather than reporting zero coverage.
The existing C0/C1, bare-CR and exact-Unicode admission rules remain in
place. This repairs coverage diagnostics; it does not implement a complete
semantic notebook, custom ruler sampling, or implicit glyph synthesis.

## Initial UTF-8 BOM in native coverage audits (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native read-only glyph and handwriting inventory reports now ignore
exactly one initial U+FEFF as source-file metadata, matching the bounded
Markdown/plain-text flow importer. Previously a BOM-prefixed bilingual
source reported a nonexistent missing ink shape and outside-baseline glyph;
BOM-only source was also incorrectly admitted as visible content.

A red-before/green-after native regression proves that an initial BOM and
Unicode line separator do not inflate two exact NFC/NFD text graphemes,
while a later U+FEFF remains a distinct auditable identity. Structural-only
sources refuse, and both commands still count the ignored BOM toward the
original 4,096-byte input cap. This does not add a font, synthesize ink, or
claim complete multilingual notebook acceptance.

## Structural Unicode separators cannot be pen glyphs (partial)

The native exact-glyph CLI now refuses U+2028 and U+2029 as authored glyph
stroke identities and as direct `--place-text` characters. These same
separators are structural line or paragraph boundaries in imported flow
and are intentionally omitted from read-only glyph inventory reports.
Previously explicit strokes could render ink for characters the coverage
audit ignored, yielding a false diagnostic-to-output correspondence.

A red-before/green-after Rust regression verifies both separators refuse,
with and without an explicitly authored separator shape, before replacing
an existing PDF. Other caller-authored Unicode spaces, including NBSP and
thin space, remain legitimate distinct glyphs. This is a local admission
consistency fix, not a complete language layout or font implementation.

Saved glyph TSV regression coverage also loads both refused separators
through the native library parser and PDF export path. Refusal preserves
the selected input TSV and previously accepted PDF bytes.

## Seeded physical ruler variation for paper-only exports (partial) — 2026-11-13

The native `atrament-paper` CLI now accepts a paired export-only
`--ruler-seed UINT32 --ruler-error-mm 0.001..0.250` selection for `ruled`
and `grid` paper. It draws four joined physical ink segments per nominal
rule, with three deterministic interior normal offsets. The exact profile
spacing, rule anchors, end coordinates and writable-area clearance remain
authoritative. The existing typed ruler-sample validator admits every
chosen displacement against the selected bounded appearance envelope.

The seed, page ordinal, direction, nominal line index and sample ordinal
produce a replayable bounded pattern. A two-page PDF varies its individual
page rules, while a selected PNG uses the exact same page-one vector path
construction. The 10,000-path output budget applies after each rule is
split into four rasterizer-compatible segments. Density or output-budget
excess refuses before a chosen output is replaced.

Five real Rust tests exercise ruled and grid physical bounds, unchanged
nominal endpoints, distinct seeds and pages, byte-identical replay of
PDF/PNG, strict paired flag and physical-error validation, rejection of
unsupported blank/dotted styles, saved-profile input, and selected PDF/PNG
preservation on refused exports. Existing paper-profile v1 bytes and legacy
PDF/PNG outputs are unchanged. Variation cannot be saved into a paper v1
profile or combined with `--emit-profile`.

Example: `atrament-paper --style ruled --ruler-seed 2026
--ruler-error-mm 0.150 --pages 2 --output varied.pdf`.

This is reproducible *synthetic* ruler variation, not a physically measured
or calibrated pen-path model. It does not add a browser ruler editor, change
the shared page-profile schema, implement bilingual line breaking or supply
missing-glyph fallbacks. The P2-013 end-to-end criterion remains open.

## English/Spanish punctuation ties for long words (partial) — 2026-11-13

The native `atrament-profiles notebook` plain/Markdown flow compositor now
keeps Spanish `¿`/`¡` openers with their following exact Unicode grapheme
and common closing punctuation with its preceding grapheme when a long
unspaced word would otherwise wrap through those pairs. Consecutive closing
marks form the same measured group. The physical row's actual pen-contour,
384-byte and 96-grapheme limits still win; when an indivisible group cannot
fit the selected row or a narrowed continuation, the compositor reuses the
previous individual-grapheme fallback rather than rejecting otherwise
printable source text. Ordinary ASCII-space word breaks and authored
NFC/NFD glyph identities are unchanged.

A red-before/green-after native test proves physical boundary corrections
in plain and Markdown flow. A deterministic 512-sample bilingual oracle
checks exact source reconstruction and unstranded opening/closing marks
across different physical widths, including decomposed `n` plus tilde.
Real native PDF and transparent PNG exports of selected text compare
byte-for-byte to independently placed authored pen strokes; missing
inverted-question ink refuses before replacing either export type. The
full source-flow test module also passes, including narrow ordered-list
cases that initially exposed a regression in strict group admission.

This is bounded visual punctuation protection within overflowing words,
not dictionary hyphenation, locale identification, semantic language tags,
full Unicode line-breaking conformance or persisted notebook flow. Exact
unsupported glyphs still need authored shapes or an explicit chosen
fallback. The broader P2-013 acceptance criterion remains open.

## Smart quotation marks and bracket ties in native flow (partial)

The CLI's opt-in long-word punctuation protection now keeps authored Spanish
`«` and English curly `“`/`‘` opening quotes, plus `(`/`[`/`{`, beside
following ink. Curly `”`/`’` closing quotes and the existing `)`/`]`/`}`
remain attached to preceding ink when a long unspaced word wraps. This
also protects nested opening and closing sequences without changing exact
NFC/NFD grapheme identities or the selected physical row's limits. Straight
ASCII quotes are deliberately left ambiguous rather than silently treated
as opening or closing punctuation.

A red-before/green-after test checks ten targeted physical boundary cases
in both plain and Markdown flow. The seeded Unicode oracle now covers
768 deterministic cases across eight quoted/bracketed or inverted pairs.
A deliberately narrow 5-mm text region also proves two-glyph clusters
fall back to original grapheme-by-grapheme placement rather than making
previously printable text refuse. Ten real-flow samples compare PDF and
transparent PNG bytes against independent exact authored-ink placements;
missing smart-quote glyphs still refuse without replacing selected output.

This improves bounded visual line breaks, not automatic fonts, dictionary
hyphenation, mixed-direction text, semantic notebook persistence or full
Unicode line-breaking rules. The P2-013 end-to-end task remains open.

## Notebook PDF/PNG integration with synthetic ruler variation (partial)

The native profile-editor notebook CLI now composes deterministic paper-only
ruler perturbations beneath the explicitly authored semantic flow and manual
pen overlay. Seed and decimal error must be supplied together for PDF/PNG;
preprinted or unsupported paper patterns refuse. The selected PNG page and
multi-page vector PDF share page-indexed physical ruling paths without
mutating notebook/paper v1 or replacing output on refusal. Unselected output
keeps exact historical byte parity.

This is output composition only. Persisted semantic block identities,
image overlays, font shape inference and complete P2-013 remain open.

## Exact nonbreaking grapheme joins in native flow (partial) — 2026-11-13

Native `atrament-profiles notebook` plain/Markdown flow now preserves
U+00A0, U+202F and U+2011 with both adjacent graphemes while breaking
unspaced long words. This only changes the advisory measured-row grouping:
when a group exceeds physical width or source-row limits, the prior
single-grapheme fallback remains. The exact Unicode identity is never
rewritten as an implicit ASCII space, and missing authored pen shapes
still refuse output without replacing the selected file.

Red-before/green-after physical-wrap tests cover all three joiners.
A deterministic 384-sample NFC/NFD matrix exercises both input modes,
source reconstruction, region limits and the no-orphan property. Narrow
5-mm rows prove fallback. Independent authored-stroke placement compares
real PDF and 72-DPI transparent PNG bytes across 12 Unicode/mode/format
combinations, with missing-glyph no-clobber assertions.

This does not introduce automatic whitespace ink, language detection,
word-break standards conformance, dictionary hyphenation or persisted
semantic notebook content. P2-013 remains open.

## Compound-word dashes avoid orphaned physical rows (partial)

The CLI flow compiler now attaches an authored ASCII `-`, U+2010,
U+2013 or U+2014 after its preceding grapheme inside one long unspaced
word. This avoids an orphan dash at the beginning of a wrapped row while
allowing a measured break immediately *after* the dash. Existing Markdown
block syntax, Unicode source identity, invisible-space policy, pen bounds
and first-row/continuation limits are unchanged. Unfit clusters fall back
to exact one-grapheme ink, without inventing dictionary hyphenation.

Four direct failing-before/fixed-after cases, 384 reproducible sampled
NFC/NFD compounds in each plain/Markdown mode, and 5-mm fallback checks
confirm no lost ink. Independently placed pen strokes reproduce exact
native vector PDF and 72-DPI transparent PNG bytes for all four dashes;
missing authored dash ink refuses without replacing accepted exports.

This is advisory visual wrapping, not a Unicode line-break standard,
logical bidirectional layout, semantic persistence or automatic dashes.
P2-013 remains open.

## Single-glyph ellipsis row protection (partial)

The native overlong-word cluster policy also prevents an authored U+2026
ellipsis from beginning a continuation row when it fits after the preceding
grapheme. A break after the mark is permitted. The same exact source is
retained through plain and Markdown flow without inferred dots or glyph
substitution. Narrow 5-mm rows safely retain single-grapheme fallback.

One red-before/green-after physical wrap, a 384-seed mixed punctuation
NFC/NFD matrix, and real manual-stroke PDF/PNG byte comparisons now include
the Unicode ellipsis. Missing authored ellipsis ink cannot replace an
accepted export. This is still not a locale or full Unicode word breaker.

